# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text The storm took the prime zone overnight, so the fleet goes down at the backup site, into the brown, with radar for height and nothing yet for place. Then Cargo 2, answered four days out, turns on every floodlight it has: a white smudge right where the terrain map says it should be. Seven ships land three kilometres off. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text Holding the plan means flying the last kilometres blind. The radar ranges on fifty metres of steel; out of the brown, every eleven seconds, a light, still asking. Seven ships land three kilometres off. Two sols later the first suit out climbs the crater rim, the first line of sight to Cargo 2's antenna, and answers from two hundred metres. The recorder scrolls up the visor, two years of REPLAY · DISCARD too fast to read, and stops on HELLO RX · OK. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, and the long orbit. Week three, night pass: seven tablets on seven galley tables, ration bars cut in half, and Cargo 2's manifest coming down a line a pass, read aloud in turn. Tonight's line is a spare drill bit, and seven people cheer a drill bit. Below, the storm's edge finally shows ground. A week later seven ships land, and the rover drives out to a ship whose floodlights hold steady. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted. Week two in orbit, on half portions; the knock is due at 03:14:22. Seven people watch the clock pass eleven seconds, then twelve, then a minute. At 03:15:40 it is back, faint in the dust, on the beat. When the sky clears the fleet lands, and the first suit out answers from the crater rim. The recorder plays aloud on the fleet channel, REPLAY · DISCARD at speed, then OK. Two of its floodlights never come back on. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air, one landing burn a long half second late, on a wind forecast cross-checked against the weather mast of Cargo 2, thirty kilometres off. Answered four days ago, it has been reporting surface wind ever since, like something that has been rehearsing. On the drive out it holds its floodlights steady and sends its whole manifest. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. On the first sol Cargo 3's ice drill snaps its only bit; the spare is on a written-off manifest, thirty kilometres off. On the third sol the rover drives out with a retuned radio and answers the next knock from two hundred metres, the first answer in two years to arrive while it's listening. The rover's screen shows one line, the last of two years of them: HELLO RX · OK. The bit is where the manifest said. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."
